-- Q1: easy / L1 — Trade discount definition
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'How is a trade discount handled in the accounting records for a sale of goods?', '["A reduction from list price that is never recorded separately in the accounting records", "A discount offered for early payment within a specified period", "A sales allowance granted after goods are damaged in transit", "A rebate returned to the buyer after reaching a volume threshold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 606, a trade discount reduces the list price to the transaction price. Only the net amount is recorded as revenue and the receivable — the discount itself receives no journal entry. Unlike cash discounts (e.g., 2/10, n/30), trade discounts are never recognized separately. Choice B is incorrect because it describes a cash (sales) discount, which is recorded when taken or anticipated, not a trade discount.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Gross method recording when cash discount is taken
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Mercer Industries sells $40,000 of merchandise on credit with terms 2/10, n/30 and uses the gross method. The customer pays within the discount period. Which entry does Mercer record upon receipt of payment?', '["Debit Cash $40,000, Credit Accounts Receivable $40,000", "Debit Cash $39,200, Debit Sales Discounts $800, Credit Accounts Receivable $40,000", "Debit Cash $39,200, Credit Accounts Receivable $39,200", "Debit Cash $39,200, Debit Allowance for Sales Discounts $800, Credit Accounts Receivable $40,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 310-10-05, the gross method records the receivable at the full invoice price ($40,000). When the customer pays within the discount window, Cash is debited for $39,200, Sales Discounts for $800 (2% × $40,000), and AR credited for $40,000. Choice C is incorrect because it would leave an $800 AR balance, failing to relieve the full receivable.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Net method forfeited discount treated as financing revenue
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Calloway Corp. sells $60,000 of goods on terms 3/10, n/30 and records the sale using the net method at $58,200. The customer pays the full $60,000 on day 25, after the discount period expires. How should Calloway record the $1,800 forfeited discount?', '["As a reduction to sales revenue", "As bad debt expense", "As interest revenue or financing revenue", "As a debit to sales discounts forfeited expense"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the net method, revenue is initially recorded at $58,200 (net of the 3% discount). When the customer pays the full $60,000 after the discount window lapses, the $1,800 excess represents financing income — the customer effectively paid for the use of money beyond the discount period (ASC 835-30 analogy). Choice A is incorrect because revenue was already recorded at the discounted amount; the forfeited discount is financing income, not a revenue adjustment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Proof of cash (four-column reconciliation) detecting irregularities
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'An auditor prepares a proof of cash (four-column bank reconciliation) for Delton Inc. After adjusting for all known timing differences — deposits in transit, outstanding checks, and identified bank errors — the beginning balances and ending balances reconcile perfectly, but an unreconciled $3,000 excess of book receipts over adjusted bank deposits remains in the receipts column. This discrepancy most likely indicates:', '["The bank failed to credit a $3,000 deposit that is not a deposit in transit", "An unrecorded bank service charge of $3,000 on cash receipts", "An NSF check for $3,000 that was returned but not yet recorded by the company", "A fictitious receipt recorded on the company''s books to conceal a cash shortage"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 305-10-05 and auditing standards, a proof of cash reconciles beginning balances, receipts, disbursements, and ending balances simultaneously, making it a powerful fraud-detection tool. When all timing differences are resolved but book receipts exceed adjusted bank deposits, a fictitious debit to cash is the most likely cause. Choice C is incorrect because an NSF check would appear as a bank disbursement, not a discrepancy in the receipts columns.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Petty cash imprest system definition
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'What is the purpose of an imprest petty cash fund?', '["To earn interest on idle cash balances", "To handle small, routine cash disbursements without writing individual checks", "To maintain a compensating balance required by a lender", "To hold restricted cash for bond sinking fund payments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 305-10-05, an imprest petty cash fund is established at a fixed amount to pay for small, routine expenditures such as postage, office supplies, or minor travel costs. When replenished, individual expense accounts are debited and Cash is credited to restore the fund to its original balance. Choice A is incorrect because petty cash is held as currency, not invested for interest.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Cash Over and Short account classification
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'What does the Cash Over and Short account represent?', '["Differences between actual cash on hand and the amount indicated by accounting records", "The cumulative balance of all bank service charges for the period", "The difference between book value and fair value of cash equivalents", "Outstanding checks that have not cleared the bank by period-end"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 305-10-05, the Cash Over and Short account captures discrepancies between actual cash counted and the amount that should be present per registers or petty cash records. A debit balance (shortage) is reported as miscellaneous expense; a credit balance (overage) is miscellaneous revenue. Choice D is incorrect because outstanding checks are a bank reconciliation timing item, not a cash count discrepancy.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Postdated check classification
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'On December 28, Hartwell Corp. receives a $15,000 check from a customer dated January 5 of the following year. How should Hartwell classify this postdated check at December 31?', '["Cash, because it is a negotiable instrument", "Cash equivalents, because it matures within 90 days", "Accounts receivable, because it cannot yet be deposited", "Notes receivable, because it is a written promise to pay"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A postdated check cannot be deposited or cashed until the date written on the check. Because Hartwell cannot convert it to cash at December 31, the check does not meet the definition of cash or a cash equivalent under ASC 305-10-05. It should remain classified as an accounts receivable until the check date arrives and the deposit clears. Choice A is incorrect because cash must be available for immediate withdrawal or use.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Bank overdraft classified as current liability
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Redford Inc. has Account A at First National Bank with a balance of $24,000 and Account B at City Savings Bank with a $3,000 overdraft. No right of offset exists between the accounts. How should the overdraft in Account B be classified?', '["Offset against Account A, reporting net cash of $21,000", "As a reduction of cash on the balance sheet", "As a negative cash equivalent", "As a current liability"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 210-20-45, a bank overdraft at a different institution is classified as a current liability when no legal right of offset exists. Netting is permitted only when both accounts are at the same bank with a formal right of setoff arrangement. Choice A is incorrect because the accounts are at different banks with no offset agreement, so netting is prohibited.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Dishonored note receivable reclassification
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Trenton Co. holds a $50,000, 90-day, 6% note receivable from a customer. On the maturity date, the customer dishonors the note. What entry should Trenton record?', '["Debit Accounts Receivable $50,750, Credit Notes Receivable $50,000, Credit Interest Revenue $750", "Debit Bad Debt Expense $50,750, Credit Notes Receivable $50,000, Credit Interest Revenue $750", "Debit Allowance for Credit Losses $50,750, Credit Notes Receivable $50,750", "Debit Accounts Receivable $50,000, Credit Notes Receivable $50,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 310-10-35, when a note is dishonored at maturity, the holder reclassifies principal plus accrued interest to Accounts Receivable, since the full amount remains legally collectible. Interest earned through maturity is $50,000 × 6% × 90/360 = $750, so A/R is debited for $50,750. Choice D is incorrect because it omits accrued interest of $750, which is legally owed and must be included in the receivable.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Net realizable value definition
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'What is the net realizable value of accounts receivable?', '["The total face amount of all outstanding invoices", "Gross accounts receivable minus the allowance for credit losses", "The discounted present value of expected future collections", "The fair market value of receivables in a secondary trading market"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 310-10-45, net realizable value (NRV) is calculated as gross accounts receivable minus the allowance for credit losses. This is the amount reported on the balance sheet and represents what the company expects to collect. Choice C is incorrect because it describes a discounted cash flow valuation used for certain long-term receivables, not the standard balance sheet presentation of trade receivables.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Effective interest method on noninterest-bearing note (Year 2 income)
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'On January 1, Year 1, Vanguard Corp. sells equipment and accepts a 2-year, noninterest-bearing note with a face value of $100,000. The present value of the note using the 10% market rate is $82,645. Vanguard uses the effective interest method. What is the interest income Vanguard recognizes in Year 2?', '["$8,264", "$8,265", "$9,091", "$10,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 835-30, a noninterest-bearing note is recorded at its present value ($100,000 ÷ 1.10² = $82,645). Year 1 interest income is $82,645 × 10% = $8,265, bringing the carrying value to $90,910. Year 2 interest is $90,910 × 10% = $9,091, amortizing the discount to $100,000 at maturity. Choice D is incorrect because $10,000 uses straight-line allocation of the $17,355 total discount over two years, rather than the effective interest method that reflects compounding.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — CECL pooling by similar risk characteristics
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Northfield Bank holds commercial and consumer loan portfolios pooled by similar risk characteristics under CECL (ASC 326). During the period, a single commercial loan deteriorates significantly and no longer shares the risk profile of its pool. How must the bank estimate expected credit losses on that loan?', '["Continue including it in the original pool using the pool''s average loss rate", "Transfer it to a newly created pool with a lower loss rate", "Write off the loan immediately since it no longer fits any pool", "Evaluate it individually on a stand-alone basis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 326-20-30-2, entities pool financial assets with similar risk characteristics for collective measurement. When an individual asset no longer shares those characteristics due to deterioration or other changes, the entity must remove it from the pool and measure expected credit losses individually. Choice A is incorrect because pooling an asset with dissimilar risk characteristics would produce an inaccurate allowance estimate.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Assignment of receivables — subsequent collection and remittance
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Waverly Corp. previously assigned $200,000 of specific receivables and received a loan of $160,000 at 12% annual interest. After one month, Waverly collects $80,000 from assigned customers and remits the collections plus one month''s interest to the finance company. What is Waverly''s total remittance?', '["$80,000", "$81,600", "$82,560", "$78,400"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 860-10-25, in a receivable assignment the assignor collects customer payments and remits them with interest on the outstanding loan balance. Monthly interest is $160,000 × 12% × 1/12 = $1,600. Total remittance is $80,000 + $1,600 = $81,600, reducing the loan to $80,000. Choice C is incorrect because $82,560 computes interest on the full assigned receivable amount ($200,000) rather than the loan balance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Factoring holdback settlement entry
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Delta Corp. factored $300,000 of receivables without recourse. The factor charged a 4% fee and withheld 10% as a holdback. After collecting all receivables in full, the factor settles the holdback. What entry does Delta record upon receiving the holdback?', '["Debit Cash $30,000, Credit Due from Factor $30,000", "Debit Cash $30,000, Credit Gain on Sale of Receivables $30,000", "Debit Cash $18,000, Credit Due from Factor $18,000", "Debit Cash $30,000, Credit Accounts Receivable $30,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 860-20-25, when receivables are factored, the holdback (10% × $300,000 = $30,000) is initially recorded as Due from Factor. Upon full collection and settlement, Delta debits Cash and credits Due from Factor to close the asset. No gain arises because the holdback was already recognized. Choice B is incorrect because recording a gain would double-count the holdback that was already an asset on Delta''s books.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Sinking fund deposits are noncurrent restricted assets
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'What is the proper balance sheet classification of cash held in a bond sinking fund?', '["Cash and cash equivalents", "Current restricted cash", "Noncurrent restricted asset", "Long-term investment in debt securities"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cash held in a sinking fund for bond retirement is restricted for a specific long-term purpose and unavailable for general operations. Under ASC 230-10-50 and general presentation guidance, sinking fund cash is classified as a noncurrent restricted asset, separate from current cash and cash equivalents. Choice A is incorrect because the funds are legally restricted and not available for immediate, unrestricted use in daily operations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Short-term compensating balance classified as current restricted asset
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Belmont Corp. is required to maintain a $100,000 compensating balance at its bank under a one-year revolving credit agreement. The balance is legally restricted and cannot be withdrawn during the loan term. How should Belmont report this compensating balance?', '["Include it in unrestricted cash and cash equivalents", "Report it as a deduction from the related borrowing", "Disclose it in the notes only, with no separate classification", "Classify it separately as a current restricted asset"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A legally restricted compensating balance related to a short-term borrowing must be reported separately from unrestricted cash as a current asset, since the restriction expires within one year. Per SEC ASR No. 148 and ASC 210-10-45, legal restrictions require separate classification on the balance sheet. Choice A is incorrect because legally restricted cash cannot be combined with cash available for general use.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Concentration of credit risk disclosure requirement
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Under ASC 825-10-50, which of the following is required when an entity has a significant concentration of credit risk in its receivables?', '["Disclosure of the nature, maximum exposure, and economic characteristics of the concentration", "Recognition of an additional allowance reserve equal to 5% of the concentrated balance", "Reclassification of the concentrated receivables to a long-term asset category", "Immediate write-down of the concentrated receivables to fair value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 825-10-50 requires entities to disclose significant concentrations of credit risk from financial instruments, including the nature of the concentration (e.g., geographic, industry), maximum exposure to loss, and any collateral or guarantees. No additional reserve or reclassification is required solely due to concentration. Choice B is incorrect because GAAP prescribes no fixed percentage reserve for concentrations; the CECL allowance already reflects expected losses.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Trade vs nontrade receivables classification
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Baxter Inc. has the following receivables: $500,000 due from customers for goods sold on credit, $45,000 owed by an employee under a payroll advance, and $80,000 due from an insurance company for a casualty claim. Which amount represents trade receivables?', '["$625,000", "$500,000", "$545,000", "$580,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Trade receivables arise from the normal sale of goods or services on credit — only the $500,000 due from customers qualifies. Employee advances ($45,000) and insurance claims ($80,000) are nontrade receivables because they do not arise from revenue-generating activities. Under ASC 310, trade and nontrade receivables are presented separately. Choice C is incorrect because it adds the employee advance, while Choice D wrongly adds the insurance claim.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Stale outstanding check reversal
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'During a bank reconciliation, Ellis Corp. discovers that a $4,200 check issued to a vendor eight months ago has not cleared the bank. The vendor confirms it never received the check. How should Ellis account for this stale check?', '["Continue listing it as an outstanding check indefinitely", "Debit Accounts Payable $4,200, Credit Cash $4,200", "Debit Cash $4,200, Credit Accounts Payable $4,200", "Debit Cash $4,200, Credit Miscellaneous Revenue $4,200"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 405-20-40, a check outstanding for an extended period (typically six months or more) is considered stale. When the company determines it will not be presented, the original disbursement is reversed by debiting Cash and crediting Accounts Payable to restore the obligation, then a replacement is reissued. Choice A is incorrect because carrying a stale check indefinitely misstates both the cash balance and the reconciliation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — CECL zero allowance must be affirmatively supported
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Highland Corp. holds trade receivables from a single investment-grade customer with a 20-year history of zero losses. Management proposes recording a zero allowance for credit losses under CECL. The external auditor challenges this position. Which statement best supports the auditor''s challenge?', '["CECL requires a minimum 1% allowance regardless of historical performance", "A zero allowance is automatically acceptable whenever historical losses are zero", "CECL applies only after a loss event has occurred, so no allowance is needed before default", "CECL requires consideration of forward-looking forecasts, so zero expected losses must be an affirmative, documented conclusion — not merely an assumption from past experience"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 326-20-30-1, the CECL model requires entities to consider historical experience, current conditions, AND reasonable and supportable forecasts. A zero allowance is not prohibited but cannot rely solely on a zero historical loss rate — management must affirmatively conclude, with documentation, that no loss is expected after evaluating all three inputs. Choice A is incorrect because GAAP prescribes no minimum percentage; Choice C describes the old incurred loss model, not CECL.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — ASC 860 three conditions for sale treatment
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Pacific Finance Co. purchases receivables from Crawford Corp. To determine whether Crawford can derecognize the receivables and record the transfer as a sale, ASC 860-10-40-5 requires that three conditions be met. Which of the following is NOT one of the required conditions?', '["The transferred assets must generate a minimum yield or return for the transferee", "The transferred assets are legally isolated from the transferor, even in bankruptcy", "The transferee has the right to pledge or exchange the transferred assets without constraint", "The transferor does not maintain effective control over the transferred assets through a repurchase agreement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 860-10-40-5 requires three conditions for sale treatment: (1) legal isolation from the transferor even in bankruptcy, (2) the transferee can pledge or exchange the assets without constraint, and (3) the transferor does not maintain effective control (e.g., no repurchase agreement). There is no requirement regarding minimum yield. Choice A is incorrect because it introduces a profitability condition that does not exist in the standard.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — CECL reversion to historical loss rates beyond forecast period
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Lakeview Bank uses a two-year reasonable and supportable forecast period for its CECL estimate on a five-year loan portfolio. For the remaining contractual life beyond the forecast horizon, Lakeview should:', '["Assume zero credit losses for years three through five", "Revert to historical loss information, either immediately or on a gradual straight-line basis", "Extend the most recent forecast rate unchanged through maturity", "Exclude years beyond the forecast horizon from the allowance estimate entirely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 326-20-30-9, when an entity cannot develop reasonable and supportable forecasts for the full contractual life, it must revert to historical loss information for the remaining period. The reversion may be immediate or gradual (e.g., straight-line). Choice D is incorrect because CECL requires estimating losses over the full remaining life — excluding years beyond the forecast horizon would understate the allowance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Write-off has no effect on net realizable value
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Pinnacle Inc. has gross accounts receivable of $800,000 and an allowance for credit losses of $32,000. The company writes off a specific $12,000 uncollectible account. What is the net realizable value of accounts receivable after the write-off?', '["$780,000", "$756,000", "$768,000", "$788,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the allowance method (ASC 326), a write-off reduces both gross receivables and the allowance by the same $12,000 — leaving net realizable value unchanged. After the write-off: gross AR = $788,000, allowance = $20,000, NRV = $768,000, which equals the pre-write-off NRV of $800,000 − $32,000 = $768,000. Choice A is incorrect because $780,000 subtracts the write-off from the original NRV, rather than recognizing that both AR and the allowance decrease equally.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L3 — Notes receivable impairment under CECL using DCF method
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'On January 1, Oakmont Bank originates a $500,000, 3-year note with a 6% stated rate requiring annual payments of $187,055. At inception, using the DCF method under CECL, the bank projects it will collect only 95% of each contractual payment. Using the original 6% effective interest rate to discount expected cash flows, what is the day-one CECL allowance?', '["$9,353", "$18,706", "$28,059", "$25,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 326-20-30-4, the DCF method measures the allowance as the difference between amortized cost and the present value of expected cash flows discounted at the original effective rate. Expected payment = 95% × $187,055 = $177,702. Since each payment is uniformly reduced by 5%, the PV of expected cash flows is 95% × $500,000 = $475,000. The allowance is $500,000 − $475,000 = $25,000. Choice A is incorrect because it applies the 5% loss rate to only one year''s payment rather than to the full amortized cost.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Bank reconciliation with unresolved difference requiring investigation
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'An auditor reconciles Quinn Corp.''s cash at December 31. Bank balance: $160,000; book balance: $152,000; deposits in transit: $12,000; outstanding checks: $18,000; bank error overcharging Quinn: $3,000; service charges: $200; note collected by bank: $6,000 (including $500 interest); NSF check: $1,800. After adjustments, the adjusted bank balance is $157,000 but the adjusted book balance is $156,000. Which conclusion about the $1,000 difference is most appropriate?', '["The company likely has an unrecorded cash receipt or an overstated disbursement on its books requiring investigation", "The difference is immaterial and can be ignored without further analysis", "The bank must have made an additional error and should adjust its records", "The NSF check was double-counted and should be removed from the reconciliation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 305-10-05, when a bank reconciliation does not balance after applying all known items, the remaining discrepancy indicates an error or unrecorded item. A $1,000 excess of adjusted bank over adjusted book suggests the company''s records understate cash — likely an unrecorded receipt or an overstated check. The auditor must investigate. Choice B is incorrect because reconciliation differences must always be resolved — they may indicate fraud or systemic errors.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Initial receivable measurement with trade discount under ASC 606
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Canton Corp. sells goods to a customer for a list price of $50,000 with a 10% trade discount and payment terms of 1/10, n/30. Using the gross method, at what amount should Canton initially record the accounts receivable?', '["$50,000", "$45,000", "$44,550", "$40,500"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 606, the transaction price reflects the list price less any trade discounts: $50,000 × (1 − 10%) = $45,000. Trade discounts are never recorded separately — only the net amount enters the books. Under the gross method, the cash discount (1/10) is not deducted until payment. Choice C is incorrect because $44,550 deducts both the trade discount and the cash discount at inception, rather than deferring the cash discount until payment is received.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Cash equivalent three-month criterion measured from purchase date
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Under ASC 230, the three-month criterion for classifying an investment as a cash equivalent is measured from which date?', '["The issuer''s original issue date", "The beginning of the entity''s fiscal year", "The date the entity purchases the investment", "The balance sheet reporting date"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 230-10-45-6 defines cash equivalents as investments with original maturities of three months or less from the date the entity purchases them. A Treasury bill with a six-month term at issuance but purchased with 60 days remaining qualifies because the holder''s purchase date governs classification. Choice D is incorrect because the classification is determined at acquisition, not reassessed at each reporting date.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Credit card receivable recorded net of processing fee
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'Taylor Boutique accepts a credit card sale of $2,000. The credit card company charges a 3% processing fee and remits the net amount within two days. What amount does Taylor record as the receivable from the credit card company at the time of sale?', '["$2,060", "$2,000", "$1,970", "$1,940"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 606-10-32, revenue is recorded at $2,000, but the receivable from the credit card company is the net amount after the 3% processing fee: $2,000 − $60 = $1,940. The $60 fee is recognized as credit card discount expense. Choice B is incorrect because it ignores the processing fee; the credit card company will remit only $1,940, so that is the receivable amount.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Allowance for credit losses is a contra asset
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'How is the allowance for credit losses classified on the balance sheet?', '["As a contra asset that reduces gross accounts receivable", "As a current liability representing estimated future payments", "As an expense reported on the income statement", "As a component of stockholders'' equity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 326 and ASC 310-10-45, the allowance for credit losses is a contra asset deducted from gross receivables to arrive at net realizable value. It is not a liability because it does not represent an obligation to pay a third party. Choice C is incorrect because it confuses the allowance (a balance sheet contra asset) with credit loss expense (an income statement charge).', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Deposit in transit definition
(2, 'Cash and Receivables', 'What is a deposit in transit in the context of a bank reconciliation?', '["A wire transfer sent to a foreign bank that has not yet arrived", "A cash deposit recorded by the company but not yet credited by the bank", "A check from a customer that has been returned for insufficient funds", "An electronic payment deducted by the bank but not yet recorded by the company"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 305-10-05, a deposit in transit is cash the company has recorded as a debit to its Cash account but that has not yet been processed and credited by the bank — typically made near the end of a business day. It is added to the bank balance in the reconciliation. Choice C is incorrect because it describes an NSF check, which is a book-side adjustment that reduces the book balance.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
